{
  "version": 1,
  "site": {
    "title": "A2CS",
    "subtitle": "Exam Prep"
  },
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "file-org",
      "title": "File Organization",
      "subtitle": "Methods of storing data in files. Determines how records are physically arranged on storage media.",
      "pdf": "A2Cs FO.pdf",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "h2",
          "text": "Types of File Organization"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "style": "ol",
          "items": [
            "Serial",
            "Sequential",
            "Random"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "1. Serial File Organization",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "Simplest method of file organization",
                "Data is stored as it comes",
                "Data is <strong>ALWAYS appended</strong> (added to the end of the file)",
                "Follows chronological order"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "text": "Examples: Text files, transaction records, log files"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "2. Sequential File Organization",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "Data is stored based on <strong>key field sequentially</strong>",
                "Key fields have to be <strong>unique</strong> AND in <strong>order</strong>"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "tablePair",
              "left": {
                "header": "Primary Key",
                "cells": [
                  "208",
                  "201",
                  "205"
                ]
              },
              "right": {
                "header": "Key Field",
                "cells": [
                  "201",
                  "205",
                  "208"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "<strong>HITRATE:</strong> The number of files that can be accessed at one moment."
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "Has <strong>high hitrate</strong> → Can apply batch processing",
                "Adding new data → Re-arrange memory → Time and power-consuming process"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "details",
              "summary": "Insertion Logic & Performance",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "note",
                  "text": "<strong>Important:</strong> Sequential files <strong>have to be in order</strong>. Inserting data in the middle means moving <strong>ALL data after the insertion point</strong> by one position."
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Adding new data requires re-arranging memory, which is time and power-consuming."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "3. Random File Organization",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "Data is stored randomly",
                "Records are stored at an address generated by a <strong>hashing algorithm</strong> applied to the key field",
                "<strong>HITRATE:</strong> Has a <strong>low hitrate</strong> → Few records can be accessed at a time"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "text": "Examples: ATMs, files with usernames and passwords"
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Collision"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "A collision occurs when one data index is assigned the same memory address as another index."
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Reasons for a Collision"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ol",
              "items": [
                "A record is <strong>already written</strong> at the address given by the hashing algorithm",
                "Two or more records are matched to the <strong>same hash address</strong>",
                "The number of key fields is greater than the number of memory addresses"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "details",
              "summary": "Solutions for Collisions",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "h3",
                  "text": "Closed Hashing"
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Move line by line to find the first available free space."
                },
                {
                  "type": "h3",
                  "text": "Open Hashing (Chaining)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Also known as <strong>Chaining</strong>. Create a separate <strong>linked list</strong> of all elements that are in collision."
                },
                {
                  "type": "h3",
                  "text": "Overflow Area"
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "A separate memory area assigned for elements in collision."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "File Access Methods",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "1. Sequential Access"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Move element by element to access records. Used with <strong>Serial</strong> and <strong>Sequential</strong> file organizations."
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "2. Direct Access"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Apply a <strong>hashing algorithm</strong> to the key field. An address is generated and the system directly accesses the required record. <strong>FASTEST method to access data</strong>."
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "If the record is not available, linearly move from the hash address until:"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ol",
              "items": [
                "Record is found",
                "Empty space is found",
                "Last address is reached"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "<strong>Used with:</strong> Sequential and Random file organizations"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "real-numbers",
      "title": "Real Numbers",
      "subtitle": "Binary representation of decimals using mantissa and exponent.",
      "pdf": "A2Cs RN.pdf",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "h2",
          "text": "Overview"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Real numbers can be represented in binary using a few key techniques:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "style": "ul",
          "items": [
            "Converting denary values to binary",
            "Handling repeating decimals",
            "Using two's complement for signed values",
            "Storing values using mantissa and exponent form"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "1. Denary to Binary Conversion",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "To convert a real number to binary:"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ol",
              "items": [
                "Convert the integer part to binary",
                "Multiply the fractional part by 2",
                "Record the integer part as the next binary bit",
                "Repeat until the fractional part becomes 0 or a repeating cycle is detected"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "details",
              "summary": "Example: 7.25",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "The integer part is 7: 7 in binary = <strong>111</strong>"
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "The fractional part is 0.25:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "style": "ul",
                  "items": [
                    "0.25 × 2 = 0.5 → bit = 0",
                    "0.5 × 2 = 1.0 → bit = 1"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "headers": [
                    "4",
                    "2",
                    "1",
                    ".",
                    "1/2",
                    "1/4",
                    "1/8"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "1",
                      "1",
                      "1",
                      ".",
                      "0",
                      "1",
                      "0"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "note",
                  "text": "Result: 7.25 = <strong>111.01</strong>"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "details",
              "summary": "Example: Repeating Decimal (10.7)",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "10 in binary = <strong>1010</strong>. Fractional part: 0.7"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "headers": [
                    "Step",
                    "Calculation",
                    "Bit"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "1",
                      "0.7 × 2 = 1.4",
                      "1"
                    ],
                    [
                      "2",
                      "0.4 × 2 = 0.8",
                      "0"
                    ],
                    [
                      "3",
                      "0.8 × 2 = 1.6",
                      "1"
                    ],
                    [
                      "4",
                      "0.6 × 2 = 1.2",
                      "1"
                    ],
                    [
                      "5",
                      "0.2 × 2 = 0.4",
                      "0"
                    ],
                    [
                      "6",
                      "0.4 × 2 = 0.8",
                      "0 → repeats"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Therefore: 10.7 ≈ <strong>1010.101100</strong>"
                },
                {
                  "type": "note",
                  "text": "The repeating section is ignored after one cycle, so the answer is shown as a recurring binary pattern."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "2. Two's Complement",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Two's complement is used to represent signed numbers in binary, especially when handling floating-point mantissas."
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Method 1: Flip & Add 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ol",
              "items": [
                "Flip every bit",
                "<strong>+ 1</strong>"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Method 2: Place-Value Table (method used in the examples below)"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Same as normal binary place values, <strong>except the leftmost (Most Significant) bit is negative.</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Convert -47 to 8-bit Two's Complement"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "-128",
                "64",
                "32",
                "16",
                "8",
                "4",
                "2",
                "1"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "1",
                  "1",
                  "0",
                  "1",
                  "0",
                  "0",
                  "0",
                  "1"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "text": "-128 + 64 + 16 + 1 = -47  →  <strong>11010001</strong>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "3. Mantissa and Exponent Method",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Computers cannot store decimals directly, so real numbers are stored as a <strong>mantissa</strong> and an <strong>exponent</strong>. This is the method used in floating-point representation."
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Mantissa"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "The mantissa contains the significant digits of the number."
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "It represents the <strong>precision</strong> of the value",
                "More digits usually means greater precision"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Example: 5.25 and 5.2575, the second value has more precision because it contains more digits after the decimal point."
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Exponent"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "It represents the <strong>range</strong> of the number",
                "It is related to the position of the decimal point"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Convert +47.25 (12-bit Mantissa, 5-bit Exponent)"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "First, convert 47.25 to binary:"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "-128",
                "64",
                "32",
                "16",
                "8",
                "4",
                "2",
                "1",
                ".",
                "1/2",
                "1/4",
                "1/8",
                "1/16"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "0",
                  "0",
                  "1",
                  "0",
                  "1",
                  "1",
                  "1",
                  "1",
                  ".",
                  "0",
                  "1",
                  "0",
                  "0"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "This gives: 47.25 = <strong>101111.01</strong> in binary"
            },
            {
              "type": "details",
              "summary": "Final Mantissa & Exponent",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "h3",
                  "text": "Final Mantissa"
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Move the decimal point so it is just before the first 1:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "bits",
                  "cells": [
                    "0",
                    ".",
                    "1",
                    "0",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "0",
                    "1",
                    "0",
                    "0",
                    "0"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "The decimal point moves 6 places to the left, so the exponent is 6."
                },
                {
                  "type": "h3",
                  "text": "Final Exponent"
                },
                {
                  "type": "bits",
                  "cells": [
                    "0",
                    "0",
                    "1",
                    "0",
                    "0"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "note",
                  "text": "mantissa = 0.10111101000  and  exponent = 6"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "4. Normalization",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Why it is needed"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Floating-point numbers cannot always be stored exactly because the computer has a limited number of bits."
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "1/3 = 0.333333333...",
                "A stored value is usually slightly higher or lower than the real value",
                "This is called <strong>rounding error</strong>"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Examples:"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "text": "0.1 + 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.29999999999999999...\n0.1 + 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.30000000000000001..."
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Normalized form"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Normalization reduces error and makes the representation more accurate. The important rule is that <strong>the first two bits of the mantissa cannot be the same</strong>:"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Normalized",
                "Meaning"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "01",
                  "valid"
                ],
                [
                  "10",
                  "valid"
                ],
                [
                  "00",
                  "not normalized"
                ],
                [
                  "11",
                  "not normalized"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "text": "Normalization is basically the process of adjusting the decimal point so the number is stored in the most efficient and accurate format."
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Why normalization matters"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "Keeps the mantissa accurate",
                "Avoids losing bits",
                "Removes multiple representations of the same number",
                "Helps represent very large and very small numbers more reliably"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "details",
              "summary": "Example: Normalize a mantissa",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "h3",
                  "text": "Before normalization"
                },
                {
                  "type": "bits",
                  "cells": [
                    "0",
                    ".",
                    "0",
                    "0",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "0"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "This starts with 00, which is not normalized."
                },
                {
                  "type": "h3",
                  "text": "Correct position for the decimal point"
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Move the decimal point to the left until the mantissa begins with 01 or 10, this is the normalized form."
                },
                {
                  "type": "bits",
                  "cells": [
                    "0",
                    ".",
                    "1",
                    "0",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "0"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "h3",
                  "text": "Exponent example"
                },
                {
                  "type": "bits",
                  "cells": [
                    "0",
                    "0",
                    "0",
                    "1",
                    "1",
                    "0",
                    "0",
                    "0"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "This value is 24 in binary, so the exponent is adjusted by the number of places the decimal point moved."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "h3",
              "text": "Final rule"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "Move the decimal point between the first two bits of the mantissa",
                "Adjust the exponent by the same number of places moved",
                "If you move <strong>left</strong>, exponent <strong>increases</strong>",
                "If you move <strong>right</strong>, exponent <strong>decreases</strong>"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "Quick Summary",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "Denary numbers are converted to binary by splitting integer and fractional parts",
                "Repeating decimals are stopped after one cycle",
                "Two's complement is used for signed integer values",
                "Mantissa and exponent store real numbers in a compact form",
                "Normalization keeps the number accurate and avoids wasted or repeated representations"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "data-types",
      "title": "User Defined Data Types",
      "subtitle": "Custom data types to reduce code complexity when built-in types are insufficient.",
      "pdf": "A2Cs UDD.pdf",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "A <strong>user created datatype</strong> that can be customized."
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "style": "ul",
          "items": [
            "Good for when software does not provide a datatype",
            "Reduces code complexity"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "h2",
          "text": "Non-Composite"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "<strong>Non Composite:</strong> Can hold <strong>ONE value</strong>, does <strong>NOT depend</strong> on any other datatypes."
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "style": "ul",
          "items": [
            "<strong>Built-in:</strong> STRING, CHAR, INTEGER, REAL, BOOLEAN",
            "<strong>User Defined:</strong> Enumerated, Pointer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "Enumerated",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "A list that is <strong>ALWAYS in order</strong> / an ordered list of elements."
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "text": "TYPE Day = (Mon,Tue,Wed,Thu,Fri,Sat,Sun)\n\nDECLARE MyDay : Day\nMyDay <-- Tue"
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "text": "REMEMBER: string values are NOT enclosed in quotes or commas here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "Pointers",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Pointers point to memory locations."
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Memory Location",
                "Values"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "101",
                  "Ayan"
                ],
                [
                  "102",
                  "Hassan"
                ],
                [
                  "103",
                  "Haji"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Let's say the pointer is at memory location 101 and is called <code>Name</code>:"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "OUTPUT <code>Name</code> → outputs <strong>Ayan</strong>",
                "OUTPUT <code>^Name</code> → outputs <strong>101</strong> (memory location / address)",
                "OUTPUT <code>Name^</code> → outputs <strong>Ayan</strong> (the value at that location)"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "When a pointer points toward a <strong>memory location</strong> (<code>^Name</code>). When a pointer points toward a <strong>value</strong> and not a memory location (<code>Name^</code>)."
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "text": "TYPE MyPointer = ^INTEGER"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Makes a pointer that points to an integer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "h2",
          "text": "Composite"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "<strong>Composite:</strong> Holds multiple values, same or different."
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "style": "ul",
          "items": [
            "<strong>Built-in:</strong> Array, Linked List, Stack, Queue",
            "<strong>User Defined:</strong> Record, CLASS, SET"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "Record",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Collection of different datatypes represented as one identifier."
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "text": "TYPE Student\n    DECLARE Surname : STRING\n    DECLARE Age : INTEGER\n    DECLARE Mark : REAL\nENDTYPE\n\nDECLARE MyStudent : Student\nMyStudent.Surname <-- \"Esa\"\nMyStudent.Age <-- 15\nMyStudent.Mark <-- 90.5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "Class",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "A collection of <strong>attributes</strong> and <strong>methods</strong> having different <strong>relationships</strong> applied to them."
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "ul",
              "items": [
                "Used in Object Oriented Programming (OOP)",
                "We'll cover this later in Classes"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "card",
          "title": "Set",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Unordered list of elements to which we apply different <strong>mathematical operations</strong>."
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "text": "Example: A, \"A\""
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}